## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a privacy-focused, offline-first synchronization system that balances security, performance, and reliability across Android process boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- Android isolatedProcess for secure service isolation
- AIDL interface design for cross-process communication
- SQLCipher integration for encrypted local storage
- Delta synchronization algorithms and conflict resolution
- Per-note access control lists and permission management
- WorkManager scheduling with network and power constraints
- Retry strategies with exponential backoff
- Comprehensive audit logging and monitoring

## Code Example

```java
// AIDL interface definition
package com.example.notes;

parcelable DeltaBatch;
parcelable SyncQuery;
interface INoteSync {
  void pushDeltaSync(in DeltaBatch batch);
  void fetchDeltaSync(in SyncQuery query);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses real-world offline-sync design, conflict handling, and security under Doze. Evaluates reasoning about data modeling, patching, and test strategy in a mobile context.

## Key Concepts
- Room data model for notes and an offline edits queue
- Delta/patch sync via REST with optimistic locking
- Pluggable ConflictResolver with a sane default
- Android Keystore per-device encryption and rotation
- OAuth token refresh and secure storage
- WorkManager orchestration with Doze/App Standby awareness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: ConflictResolver contract
class ConflictResolver {
  resolve(local, remote) {
    // default: last-write-wins
    return (local.lastModified >= remote.lastModified) ? local : remote;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge cases like out-of-order patches and token expiry?
- How would you scale the queue and ensure user-visible consistency during long offline periods?

